1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,660
Three hundred and fifty-five billion.

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:06,565
That is how many payments carried Visa's

3
00:00:06,565 --> 00:00:08,690
name in a single year.

4
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:12,740
Close to one billion every single day.

5
00:00:12,983 --> 00:00:15,071
And yet Visa does not lend you

6
00:00:15,071 --> 00:00:16,123
money.

7
00:00:16,123 --> 00:00:18,123
Neither does Mastercard.

8
00:00:18,143 --> 00:00:20,473
They did not even start as companies.

9
00:00:20,473 --> 00:00:21,819
They started as clubs,

10
00:00:21,819 --> 00:00:24,471
owned by the banks that used them.

11
00:00:24,717 --> 00:00:26,470
So how did two clubs of banks

12
00:00:26,470 --> 00:00:29,225
become the gate that almost every card

13
00:00:29,225 --> 00:00:32,580
payment in America has to pass through?

14
00:00:32,817 --> 00:00:34,999
By the end of this video,

15
00:00:34,999 --> 00:00:36,933
you will know how that gate was

16
00:00:36,933 --> 00:00:37,458
built,

17
00:00:37,458 --> 00:00:39,392
why it is so hard to go

18
00:00:39,392 --> 00:00:40,470
around it,

19
00:00:40,470 --> 00:00:42,680
and why the courts and Congress keep

20
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:44,937
trying to pry it open.

21
00:00:50,583 --> 00:00:53,030
The story starts in California,

22
00:00:53,030 --> 00:00:55,113
in nineteen fifty-eight.

23
00:00:55,213 --> 00:00:57,671
Bank of America launches a new kind

24
00:00:57,671 --> 00:00:58,319
of card,

25
00:00:58,319 --> 00:01:00,483
called BankAmericard.

26
00:01:00,703 --> 00:01:03,243
The bank does everything itself.

27
00:01:03,243 --> 00:01:05,059
It signs up the shoppers,

28
00:01:05,059 --> 00:01:06,874
it signs up the stores,

29
00:01:06,874 --> 00:01:09,053
and it carries the risk.

30
00:01:09,293 --> 00:01:10,602
It is one bank,

31
00:01:10,602 --> 00:01:13,071
running one closed system.

32
00:01:13,317 --> 00:01:16,126
But one bank can only reach so

33
00:01:16,126 --> 00:01:16,967
far.

34
00:01:16,967 --> 00:01:19,005
So in nineteen sixty-six,

35
00:01:19,005 --> 00:01:21,753
according to the Federal Reserve's own

36
00:01:21,753 --> 00:01:22,647
history,

37
00:01:22,647 --> 00:01:25,395
Bank of America starts licensing the card

38
00:01:25,395 --> 00:01:26,517
to other banks,

39
00:01:26,517 --> 00:01:29,407
in the United States and abroad.

40
00:01:29,627 --> 00:01:30,652
That same year,

41
00:01:30,652 --> 00:01:32,754
a group of rival banks forms its

42
00:01:32,754 --> 00:01:33,779
own answer,

43
00:01:33,779 --> 00:01:37,007
a cooperative called Interbank.

44
00:01:37,067 --> 00:01:39,129
In nineteen sixty-nine,

45
00:01:39,129 --> 00:01:41,817
it takes the name Master Charge.

46
00:01:41,837 --> 00:01:45,135
That is the ancestor of Mastercard.

47
00:01:45,383 --> 00:01:48,953
The BankAmericard system was struggling.

48
00:01:49,083 --> 00:01:51,023
In nineteen sixty-nine,

49
00:01:51,023 --> 00:01:53,723
a banker named Dee Hock gathered a

50
00:01:53,723 --> 00:01:55,933
group of bankers at a hotel in

51
00:01:55,933 --> 00:01:58,743
Sausalito to redesign it.

52
00:01:58,793 --> 00:02:01,223
The answer was radical.

53
00:02:01,333 --> 00:02:04,069
Bank of America would give the system

54
00:02:04,069 --> 00:02:05,166
away.

55
00:02:05,417 --> 00:02:07,110
In nineteen seventy,

56
00:02:07,110 --> 00:02:09,468
ownership passed to the banks that issued

57
00:02:09,468 --> 00:02:10,457
the card.

58
00:02:10,457 --> 00:02:12,276
The network became a club,

59
00:02:12,276 --> 00:02:15,246
a corporation with no shares for sale,

60
00:02:15,246 --> 00:02:17,227
owned by its members.

61
00:02:17,237 --> 00:02:19,206
In nineteen seventy-six,

62
00:02:19,206 --> 00:02:21,175
after an internal contest,

63
00:02:21,175 --> 00:02:22,701
the club chose a new name,

64
00:02:22,701 --> 00:02:24,471
one that sounds the same in almost

65
00:02:24,471 --> 00:02:26,177
every language.

66
00:02:26,397 --> 00:02:27,699
Visa.

67
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:31,120
Let me draw why the club worked.

68
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:31,952
Before,

69
00:02:31,952 --> 00:02:34,106
every bank had its own card,

70
00:02:34,106 --> 00:02:36,284
and each card worked only in its

71
00:02:36,284 --> 00:02:37,610
own stores.

72
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:39,198
Here is bank A,

73
00:02:39,198 --> 00:02:40,760
with its stores.

74
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:42,117
Here is bank B,

75
00:02:42,117 --> 00:02:43,710
with its stores.

76
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:46,710
Now put both inside one club.

77
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:49,628
Suddenly every card works in every store

78
00:02:49,628 --> 00:02:51,520
of every member.

79
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:54,224
Each bank keeps its own customers,

80
00:02:54,224 --> 00:02:56,817
but they all share one network.

81
00:02:57,083 --> 00:02:58,933
For almost forty years,

82
00:02:58,933 --> 00:03:02,213
that is what Visa and Mastercard were.

83
00:03:02,293 --> 00:03:05,143
Clubs owned by thousands of banks.

84
00:03:05,283 --> 00:03:08,168
Then the club turned into a company.

85
00:03:18,817 --> 00:03:21,087
Mastercard went first.

86
00:03:21,087 --> 00:03:22,822
In May two thousand six,

87
00:03:22,822 --> 00:03:25,086
it sold shares to the public for

88
00:03:25,086 --> 00:03:26,067
the first time,

89
00:03:26,067 --> 00:03:28,427
at thirty-nine dollars each.

90
00:03:28,527 --> 00:03:31,065
The offering raised two point four billion

91
00:03:31,065 --> 00:03:32,127
dollars.

92
00:03:32,207 --> 00:03:34,244
And most of the money went straight

93
00:03:34,244 --> 00:03:36,001
back to the old owners,

94
00:03:36,001 --> 00:03:36,740
the banks,

95
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:38,868
to buy back their shares.

96
00:03:39,117 --> 00:03:40,738
Before its own listing,

97
00:03:40,738 --> 00:03:43,558
Visa in the United States was owned

98
00:03:43,558 --> 00:03:46,142
by about fourteen thousand banks and other

99
00:03:46,142 --> 00:03:48,387
financial institutions.

100
00:03:48,507 --> 00:03:49,945
In March two thousand eight,

101
00:03:49,945 --> 00:03:52,425
in the middle of a financial crisis,

102
00:03:52,425 --> 00:03:54,037
Visa went public.

103
00:03:54,037 --> 00:03:56,042
It sold four hundred and six million

104
00:03:56,042 --> 00:03:58,647
shares at forty-four dollars each.

105
00:03:58,647 --> 00:04:00,103
By our calculation,

106
00:04:00,103 --> 00:04:02,425
that is about seventeen point nine billion

107
00:04:02,425 --> 00:04:03,447
dollars.

108
00:04:03,567 --> 00:04:05,522
Visa itself calls it one of the

109
00:04:05,522 --> 00:04:08,295
largest stock offerings in history.

110
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:09,780
Why sell?

111
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:11,700
Part of the reason was the courts.

112
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:13,820
Stores had been suing the card networks

113
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:14,503
for years,

114
00:04:14,503 --> 00:04:16,387
saying the banks inside the club were

115
00:04:16,387 --> 00:04:18,400
setting fees together.

116
00:04:18,770 --> 00:04:20,210
As public companies,

117
00:04:20,210 --> 00:04:22,924
Visa and Mastercard would be businesses in

118
00:04:22,924 --> 00:04:23,739
their own right,

119
00:04:23,739 --> 00:04:25,800
not clubs of competing banks.

120
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:28,546
Merchant lawyers even asked courts to undo

121
00:04:28,546 --> 00:04:29,795
the listings.

122
00:04:30,050 --> 00:04:32,270
Let me draw the change.

123
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:32,933
Before,

124
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:34,740
the banks owned the network,

125
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:37,130
and the network served the banks.

126
00:04:37,130 --> 00:04:37,768
After,

127
00:04:37,768 --> 00:04:40,790
the network is owned by shareholders.

128
00:04:40,870 --> 00:04:43,150
The banks become customers.

129
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:45,630
The network is now a business with

130
00:04:45,630 --> 00:04:47,005
its own interests,

131
00:04:47,005 --> 00:04:48,770
and its own share price.

132
00:04:49,017 --> 00:04:50,739
And it became a very,

133
00:04:50,739 --> 00:04:52,627
very large business.

134
00:04:52,627 --> 00:04:54,565
Because of a force that is almost

135
00:04:54,565 --> 00:04:57,104
impossible to compete against.

136
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:04,177
In twenty eighteen,

137
00:05:04,177 --> 00:05:06,595
the Supreme Court described what a card

138
00:05:06,595 --> 00:05:07,910
network is.

139
00:05:07,910 --> 00:05:10,731
It called it a two-sided platform,

140
00:05:10,731 --> 00:05:12,517
serving two groups at once,

141
00:05:12,517 --> 00:05:14,670
card holders and merchants.

142
00:05:14,750 --> 00:05:16,626
And it said these platforms have what

143
00:05:16,626 --> 00:05:19,900
economists call indirect network effects.

144
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:22,187
The network is worth more to stores

145
00:05:22,187 --> 00:05:24,449
when more shoppers carry its cards,

146
00:05:24,449 --> 00:05:26,481
and worth more to shoppers when more

147
00:05:26,481 --> 00:05:28,352
stores accept them.

148
00:05:28,617 --> 00:05:31,107
That creates a loop.

149
00:05:31,277 --> 00:05:33,837
More cards bring more stores.

150
00:05:33,837 --> 00:05:36,297
More stores bring more cards.

151
00:05:36,677 --> 00:05:39,601
The Court also noted that almost every

152
00:05:39,601 --> 00:05:42,037
bank offering credit cards is in the

153
00:05:42,037 --> 00:05:44,205
Visa or Mastercard network,

154
00:05:44,205 --> 00:05:47,729
because both began as bank cooperatives.

155
00:05:47,983 --> 00:05:49,834
Look at the size of that loop

156
00:05:49,834 --> 00:05:50,963
today.

157
00:05:51,103 --> 00:05:53,408
Visa reports five billion payment

158
00:05:53,408 --> 00:05:54,408
credentials,

159
00:05:54,408 --> 00:05:56,969
more than four hundred million places that

160
00:05:56,969 --> 00:05:57,968
accept them,

161
00:05:57,968 --> 00:06:00,530
and eighteen trillion dollars in total

162
00:06:00,530 --> 00:06:02,923
volume in its latest year.

163
00:06:03,243 --> 00:06:06,102
Mastercard reported ten point six trillion

164
00:06:06,102 --> 00:06:08,441
dollars in gross volume in twenty

165
00:06:08,441 --> 00:06:09,821
twenty-five.

166
00:06:10,083 --> 00:06:12,516
Among the big card networks,

167
00:06:12,516 --> 00:06:14,963
the gap is enormous.

168
00:06:15,123 --> 00:06:17,131
In Visa's annual report,

169
00:06:17,131 --> 00:06:19,138
citing industry data,

170
00:06:19,138 --> 00:06:21,679
Visa handled about fourteen point six

171
00:06:21,679 --> 00:06:24,221
trillion dollars in payments in twenty

172
00:06:24,221 --> 00:06:25,583
twenty-five.

173
00:06:25,703 --> 00:06:26,622
Mastercard,

174
00:06:26,622 --> 00:06:29,343
about eight point eight trillion dollars.

175
00:06:29,383 --> 00:06:31,030
American Express,

176
00:06:31,030 --> 00:06:31,961
the next one,

177
00:06:31,961 --> 00:06:34,709
about one point nine trillion dollars.

178
00:06:34,950 --> 00:06:37,010
Let me draw the moat.

179
00:06:37,180 --> 00:06:38,853
Here is a new network,

180
00:06:38,853 --> 00:06:40,180
trying to start.

181
00:06:40,250 --> 00:06:41,834
No store will accept it,

182
00:06:41,834 --> 00:06:44,500
because nobody carries the card.

183
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:46,689
Nobody will carry the card,

184
00:06:46,689 --> 00:06:48,870
because no store accepts it.

185
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:50,647
On the other side,

186
00:06:50,647 --> 00:06:52,292
Visa and Mastercard,

187
00:06:52,292 --> 00:06:54,438
with billions of cards and hundreds of

188
00:06:54,438 --> 00:06:56,230
millions of stores.

189
00:06:56,430 --> 00:06:58,263
The loop that made them big is

190
00:06:58,263 --> 00:07:00,958
the same loop that keeps others out.

191
00:07:01,217 --> 00:07:03,703
But a moat can also be dug

192
00:07:03,703 --> 00:07:05,857
deeper on purpose.

193
00:07:05,877 --> 00:07:08,224
And that is where the government stepped

194
00:07:08,224 --> 00:07:09,059
in.

195
00:07:14,717 --> 00:07:17,426
By the late nineteen nineties,

196
00:07:17,426 --> 00:07:20,436
Visa and Mastercard had rules for their

197
00:07:20,436 --> 00:07:21,837
member banks.

198
00:07:21,837 --> 00:07:23,696
A bank could issue their cards,

199
00:07:23,696 --> 00:07:26,660
but it could not issue American Express

200
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:28,607
or Discover cards.

201
00:07:28,787 --> 00:07:30,777
The government sued.

202
00:07:31,007 --> 00:07:32,602
According to its brief,

203
00:07:32,602 --> 00:07:35,144
Visa and Mastercard then accounted for

204
00:07:35,144 --> 00:07:37,918
seventy-three percent of general purpose

205
00:07:37,918 --> 00:07:39,051
card transactions,

206
00:07:39,051 --> 00:07:41,962
and eighty-five percent of cards issued.

207
00:07:42,217 --> 00:07:45,137
The courts agreed with the government.

208
00:07:45,207 --> 00:07:47,737
The rules had worked as intended.

209
00:07:47,857 --> 00:07:50,753
No bank in the continental United States

210
00:07:50,753 --> 00:07:53,204
had agreed to issue American Express

211
00:07:53,204 --> 00:07:54,027
cards.

212
00:07:54,217 --> 00:07:57,053
Visa and Mastercard were ordered to repeal

213
00:07:57,053 --> 00:07:57,467
them,

214
00:07:57,467 --> 00:07:58,972
and in two thousand three,

215
00:07:58,972 --> 00:08:01,535
the appeals court upheld that order.

216
00:08:01,783 --> 00:08:05,183
Then Congress went after debit cards.

217
00:08:05,393 --> 00:08:07,571
A law passed in twenty ten,

218
00:08:07,571 --> 00:08:09,748
known as the Durbin Amendment,

219
00:08:09,748 --> 00:08:12,684
told the Federal Reserve to limit debit

220
00:08:12,684 --> 00:08:14,263
fees for large banks.

221
00:08:14,463 --> 00:08:16,715
Today the cap is twenty-one cents,

222
00:08:16,715 --> 00:08:18,992
plus five hundredths of a percent of

223
00:08:18,992 --> 00:08:19,979
the purchase,

224
00:08:19,979 --> 00:08:22,603
plus one cent for fraud prevention.

225
00:08:23,093 --> 00:08:25,880
The law also requires every debit card

226
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:28,025
to work on at least two unrelated

227
00:08:28,025 --> 00:08:29,053
networks.

228
00:08:29,317 --> 00:08:31,118
In twenty twenty-three,

229
00:08:31,118 --> 00:08:34,327
the Fed proposed lowering that cap.

230
00:08:34,437 --> 00:08:37,094
On an average fifty dollar purchase,

231
00:08:37,094 --> 00:08:39,104
it would fall from twenty-four and a

232
00:08:39,104 --> 00:08:41,937
half cents to seventeen point seven cents.

233
00:08:42,167 --> 00:08:45,037
That proposal has still not become final.

234
00:08:45,187 --> 00:08:46,110
Meanwhile,

235
00:08:46,110 --> 00:08:48,477
two federal courts reached opposite

236
00:08:48,477 --> 00:08:51,081
decisions on whether the cap itself is

237
00:08:51,081 --> 00:08:51,767
valid,

238
00:08:51,767 --> 00:08:54,261
and for now it stays in effect.

239
00:08:54,517 --> 00:08:56,767
Europe went further.

240
00:08:56,767 --> 00:08:58,179
Since twenty fifteen,

241
00:08:58,179 --> 00:09:00,812
European Union law has capped the fee

242
00:09:00,812 --> 00:09:03,205
banks earn on consumer cards at zero

243
00:09:03,205 --> 00:09:05,599
point two percent for debit and zero

244
00:09:05,599 --> 00:09:07,874
point three percent for credit.

245
00:09:08,117 --> 00:09:10,173
Let me draw the walls of the

246
00:09:10,173 --> 00:09:11,067
gate.

247
00:09:11,287 --> 00:09:11,758
First,

248
00:09:11,758 --> 00:09:13,966
the exclusivity rules,

249
00:09:13,966 --> 00:09:15,807
torn down by the courts.

250
00:09:15,877 --> 00:09:16,545
Second,

251
00:09:16,545 --> 00:09:19,286
the debit cap and the two-network rule,

252
00:09:19,286 --> 00:09:20,797
set by Congress.

253
00:09:20,797 --> 00:09:21,332
Third,

254
00:09:21,332 --> 00:09:23,057
Europe's caps.

255
00:09:23,067 --> 00:09:25,137
Each one opened a small door.

256
00:09:25,137 --> 00:09:27,300
None of them removed the gate.

257
00:09:27,550 --> 00:09:30,474
And now the gate itself is on

258
00:09:30,474 --> 00:09:31,440
trial.

259
00:09:37,083 --> 00:09:39,451
In September twenty twenty-four,

260
00:09:39,451 --> 00:09:42,203
the Justice Department sued Visa.

261
00:09:42,433 --> 00:09:44,997
It alleges that Visa illegally protected a

262
00:09:44,997 --> 00:09:46,793
monopoly in debit.

263
00:09:47,053 --> 00:09:48,807
According to the complaint,

264
00:09:48,807 --> 00:09:50,804
more than sixty percent of debit

265
00:09:50,804 --> 00:09:53,468
transactions in America run on Visa's

266
00:09:53,468 --> 00:09:54,112
network,

267
00:09:54,112 --> 00:09:56,331
letting it charge more than seven billion

268
00:09:56,331 --> 00:09:58,263
dollars in fees a year.

269
00:09:58,413 --> 00:10:00,533
These are allegations.

270
00:10:00,763 --> 00:10:02,505
In twenty twenty-five,

271
00:10:02,505 --> 00:10:05,256
a judge refused to dismiss the case,

272
00:10:05,256 --> 00:10:07,371
and it is still going on.

273
00:10:07,617 --> 00:10:09,993
The complaint even quotes Visa's own

274
00:10:09,993 --> 00:10:12,607
description of the network effects around

275
00:10:12,607 --> 00:10:14,157
its business.

276
00:10:14,667 --> 00:10:16,795
An enormous moat.

277
00:10:17,050 --> 00:10:19,027
The stores have their own case,

278
00:10:19,027 --> 00:10:21,280
and it has run for about twenty

279
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:22,130
years.

280
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:23,490
In twenty nineteen,

281
00:10:23,490 --> 00:10:25,546
a court approved a settlement for the

282
00:10:25,546 --> 00:10:26,520
merchants.

283
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:28,028
By our arithmetic,

284
00:10:28,028 --> 00:10:30,606
after money returned for stores that opted

285
00:10:30,606 --> 00:10:30,982
out,

286
00:10:30,982 --> 00:10:32,569
it came to about five and a

287
00:10:32,569 --> 00:10:34,130
half billion dollars.

288
00:10:34,130 --> 00:10:35,230
A second deal,

289
00:10:35,230 --> 00:10:36,780
in twenty twenty-four,

290
00:10:36,780 --> 00:10:38,951
was rejected by the judge.

291
00:10:39,217 --> 00:10:41,704
A new deal was signed in November

292
00:10:41,704 --> 00:10:43,547
twenty twenty-five.

293
00:10:43,567 --> 00:10:45,989
It would cut the average credit card

294
00:10:45,989 --> 00:10:48,168
